759 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 457 0 R
/E (As this transition unfolds, the central question is no longer simply where innovation is happening. It is where innovation is being translated into systems that institutions can trust, use, and scale. The next phase of digital finance will not be determined by technological novelty alone. It will depend on whether new financial infrastructure can operate with legal clarity, regulatory credibility, operational resilience, and cross-border usability. In other words, the issue is no longer just digital transformation. It is institutionalization.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
760 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 457 0 R
/E (This is the context in which the United Arab Emirates has become increasingly significant. The UAE is not only emerging as a fast-growing hub for digital assets, fintech, and next-generation infrastructure.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
761 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 457 0 R
/E (The global financial system is entering a new phase of structural reconfiguration. For decades, cross-border finance was built on relatively stable assumptions: capital moved through established channels, trust was concentrated in a limited number of institutions, and the infrastructure supporting payments, custody, and settlement evolved gradually around legacy financial rails. That environment is changing. Geopolitical fragmentation, the redrawing of trade corridors, the acceleration of digital infrastructure, and the rise of programmable forms of value are reshaping how money moves, how assets are held, and how institutions interact across borders. These shifts are not temporary disruptions. They are redefining the operating conditions of global finance. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
